<div class="article-content prose" style="margin-top: 2.5rem; padding-top: 2rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line);">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Calculation Formula &amp; Worked Example</h2>
  <p>Because the base transfer ratio is 5:4, you receive 0.80 miles per bank point before any promotional bonus. Points must be transferred in blocks of 1,000:</p>
  
  <div class="formula-box" style="margin: 1.5rem 0; padding: 1.25rem; background: var(--ink-2); border-left: 4px solid var(--gold); border-radius: 6px;">
    <div><strong>Transferable Points</strong> = Floor(Total Points ÷ 1,000) × 1,000 (minimum 1,000)</div>
    <div style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"><strong>Asia Miles Received</strong> = (Transferable Points ÷ 1,000) × 800 × (1 + Promotional Bonus %)</div>
    <div><strong>Effective Miles per Point</strong> = 0.80 × (1 + Promotional Bonus %)</div>
    <div style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"><strong>Points Remaining in Amex</strong> = Total Points - Transferable Points</div>
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Real-World Example</h3>
  <p>Suppose you want to book a Cathay Pacific long-haul flight and have <strong>75,600 Amex Membership Rewards points</strong> with no promotional bonus:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Eligible Transferable Block</strong>: 75,000 points (600 points remain in Amex).</li>
    <li><strong>Base 5:4 Ratio Calculation</strong>: 75,000 × 0.80 = <strong>60,000 Asia Miles</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Bonus Miles</strong>: 0.</li>
    <li><strong>Total Miles Received</strong>: <strong>60,000 Asia Miles</strong> (effective multiplier: <strong>0.80</strong>).</li>
    <li><strong>Estimated Travel Value</strong>: At an illustrative baseline valuation assumption of 1.3¢ per Asia Mile, 60,000 miles represent roughly <strong>$780</strong> in modeled travel purchasing power.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Transfer Rules, Fees, and Warning for US Cardholders</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The 5:4 Ratio Consideration</strong>: Unlike 1:1 partners, transferring to Cathay yields 0.8 miles per point. You should verify that your flight redemption CPP compensates for this ratio.</li>
    <li><strong>Chase Does Not Transfer to Cathay</strong>: A common misconception is that Chase transfers to Cathay Pacific. <strong>Chase Ultimate Rewards has no direct partnership with Cathay.</strong> Amex, Citi, Capital One, and Bilt are the primary US transferable point currencies that support Asia Miles.</li>
    <li><strong>Excise Fee Offset</strong>: <strong>$0 Free</strong>. As an international carrier, Amex charges no federal excise offset fees.</li>
    <li><strong>Transfer Speed</strong>: Transfers often complete within a few minutes to hours, though official terms allow up to 24–48 hours for batch processing.</li>
    <li><strong>Strictly Non-Reversible</strong>: Transferred Asia Miles cannot normally be returned to Amex. Verify live seat availability on CathayPacific.com before transferring.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Valuation Trade-Off: When Does a 5:4 Transfer Make Sense?</h2>
  <p>Amex Membership Rewards carry an illustrative baseline valuation around <strong>1.5¢ per point</strong>, while Cathay Pacific Asia Miles average around <strong>1.3¢ per mile</strong>. Factoring in the 5:4 ratio, your breakeven benchmark threshold is:</p>
  <p style="text-align: center; font-weight: 500; color: var(--gold);">1.5¢ ÷ 0.80 = 1.88¢ per Asia Mile</p>
  <p>This means you should aim for redemptions yielding <strong>at least 1.9¢ per Asia Mile</strong> to beat the baseline opportunity cost of your Amex points. Common sweet spots that easily exceed 2.0¢–3.5¢ CPP include Cathay Pacific long-haul Business Class (e.g., Hong Kong to North America or Europe) and multi-carrier oneworld award charts.</p>

